## Problem

**Question:** After controlling for age, marital status, and group affiliation, does the probability and velocity of primary online radicalization among lone-actor terrorists increase significantly in the post-algorithmic era (2011-2022) compared to the pre-algorithmic era (1999-2010)?

Major social media platforms began using algorithms to rank and recommend content in the early 2010s. Facebook changed its ranking system around 2011 ([source](https://www.newamerica.org/oti/reports/rising-through-ranks/news-feed-ranking/)) and YouTube changed its recommendation system in 2012 ([source](https://www.newamerica.org/oti/reports/why-am-i-seeing-this/case-study-youtube)). This project uses 2011 as a rough dividing line.

This project does **not** show that algorithms caused any change. It compares two time periods on two outcomes:

1. **Probability:** the share of lone offenders whose radicalization was primarily online.
2. **Velocity:** the share who radicalized in under one year.

