## Cleaning log

| Step | Rows | Detail |
|---|---|---|
| Load raw PIRUS V5 | 3,528 | one row per individual |
| Keep lone offenders | 1,165 | `Lone_Offender == 1` |
| Keep exposure year >= 1999 | 1,091 | dropped 74; the internet variable is not applicable before 1999 |
| Define era | 1,091 | pre (1999-2010) n = 107, post (2011-2022) n = 984 |
| Recode -99 (unknown) to missing | 1,091 | Age = 14, Marital_Status = 248, Group_Membership = 11, Internet_Radicalization = 384, Rad_duration = 493 |
| Build outcomes and controls | 1,091 | `prim_online`, `fast` (< 1 year), marital status (widowed merged into divorced/separated, n = 9), group labels |
| Complete cases, main model | 607 | primary-online events = 140 |

**Variable definitions**
- `prim_online` = 1 if the internet was the *primary* means of radicalization (`Internet_Radicalization == 2`).
- `fast` = 1 if radicalization took under one year (`Rad_duration == 1`).
- Controls: age, marital status (single, married, divorced/separated), group affiliation (none, aboveground, informal, formal/movement).

## Findings

### 1. Primary online radicalization is more common in the later era

![Share radicalized primarily online](figures/fig1_share.png)

Among lone offenders with a known online-radicalization status, **22.3%** (2011-2022) were radicalized primarily online versus **11.9%** (1999-2010). The earlier group is much smaller, which is why its confidence interval is wider.

### 2. The difference holds after adjusting for age, marital status, and group affiliation

![Adjusted odds ratio](figures/fig2_main_model.png)

A logistic regression with robust (HC1) standard errors gives an adjusted **odds ratio of 3.30 (95% CI 1.00-10.84, p = 0.049)**, an average effect of about **+15.0 percentage points**. The model uses the 607 complete cases, which have 3 primary-online events out of 33 in the early era and 137 out of 574 in the later era. The result is statistically significant but imprecise and borderline, so it should be read as **suggestive, not conclusive**.

### 3. Radicalization is faster in the later era

![Radicalization duration by era](figures/fig3_velocity.png)

Among offenders with a known duration (n = 513 in the adjusted model), the share radicalized in under one year rose from **24% to 43%**. Adjusted for the same controls, the odds of radicalizing in under a year were about **2.3 times higher (OR = 2.33, 95% CI 1.17-4.65, p = 0.017)**, an average effect of about **+17.6 percentage points**.

## Limitations

- **No causal claim.** This is a comparison of two time periods, not evidence that algorithms caused the change. Platforms changed at different times, so 2011 is a rough cutoff.
- **Missing data.** The role of the internet is unknown for 60.7% of lone offenders in 1999-2010 versus 32.4% in 2011-2022 (unknown duration: 49.5% vs 44.7%). The models use complete cases only. If unknown cases differ from known ones, the comparison could be biased in a direction this analysis cannot determine. The confidence intervals and p-values reflect sampling uncertainty only.
- **Small early group.** Only 3 primary-online cases appear in the 1999-2010 complete-case sample. The main result (p = 0.049) is borderline.
- **Velocity bias.** People in the later era have had less time to offend (2011-2022 vs. 1999-2022 for the earlier window), so slow radicalizers may not have offended yet. That could make the later era look faster.

